Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-10-2001||05:43 PM||||any wholesale auto paint supply will mix any color you want usually. They also have 'Fleet' books there available that show all the colors with all of each color together going from lightest to darkest shades. If you have a sample of a color you want, they can electronicly scan it and mix the paint to match it. Usually wont cost anymore than regular colors of the same price code. Red are highest normally, with whites and black being the cheapest. Of course, anything that has mica, or pearl will be higher too. Most people call metallic colors (with little specs) metalflake. Metalflake are large flecs and a lot different and more expensive to paint (more similar to glitter) Mica is in lots of Mopar colors, sorta looks like metallic, but refracts lite to make colors change somewhat. Pearl is a coat thats applied over a color base that has a 'floresent' look like the inside of a seashell. Candies are very transparent color tints that are applied over a base color, usually gold, silver or white. Candies are also 99% impossible to match if you ever have too, as every coat thats applied makes it appear darker, so id stay away from those except on a show car, not one driven all the time. <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 08-10-2001).]||||reg||1
